Not quite. test_model_registration_and_provenance() is an integration test, but I would not call it a full E2E test if it only starts with an already-logged model URI and then tests registration.

Your description:

Model registration → version → run ID → dataset version → validation status → provenance

is excellent for a model registration/provenance integration test.

A true E2E test should start from the pipeline input and exercise the complete workflow through to the final registered model/model card retrieval.

I would name them like this
Test	Type	Scope
test_summarization_model_lifecycle()	Integration	Summarization → model logging/loading
test_model_registration_and_provenance()	Integration	Registration → version → lineage/provenance
test_mlflow_promotion()	Integration	Evaluation → promotion decision
test_model_card_logging_and_retrieval()	Integration	Model card → artifact → retrieval
test_summarization_pipeline_end_to_end()	E2E	Input → summarization → logging → registration → evaluation → promotion → model card → retrieval

So yes, your proposed test absolutely should exist, and I would keep it. But I would describe it as:

Model registration and provenance validation — verifies registered model version, source run ID, dataset version, validation status, and lineage metadata.

Not as the complete E2E test.

If your goal is to have one test that proves “everything works together,” then the E2E test should call the actual pipeline and then assert the final MLflow state, rather than independently performing each operation itself.

